  <div slot="started">
    <p>
      Honest Hank’s existed, but it wasn’t yet the fully branded, process-driven company Lewis is
      building now. He’d done the hard part of getting work as a contractor. The next part
      (looking like a company customers trust at a glance, generating consistent Google demand, and
      hiring) needed a partner who wouldn’t force a bet-the-business setup package.
    </p>
    <p>
      John at Rewired referred Lewis (out of state, Iowa referrals stay scarce for competitive
      reasons). That trust mattered: the relationship started with proof of how we grow companies
      in stages.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The point of the model</h2>
  <p>
    Every step waited on lead flow. Build something small → watch bookings rise → add the next
    piece. Brand and website weren’t day-one vanity. They arrived when Honest Hank’s could afford to
    look like a scaled shop, and when the Google foundation was already earning.
  </p>
  <p>
    That’s why moments like <strong>van three</strong> land so well: they’re not a marketing slide.
    They’re the scoreboard for a process that never asked Lewis to fund a fantasy.
  </p>
